The contract parts, verified:

```ts
// SIGNATURE — optional, defaults, rest
function clamp(n: number, min = 0, max = 100, ...tags: string[]): number {
  return Math.min(Math.max(n, min), max);
}

// FUNCTION TYPES — the callable shape
type Handler<E> = (event: E, index: number) => void;

// GENERICS — CONNECT positions (input ↔ output)
function first<T>(xs: readonly T[]): T | undefined {
  return xs[0];                       // T at the call site stays 'string'
}
// CONSTRAINT — require AND unlock:
function pluck<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];                    // T[K] = the EXACT property type
}
pluck(user, 'id');                    // returns number — not 'string | number'

// OVERLOADS — real input → output relationships
function parse(input: string): object;
function parse(input: string[]): object[];
function parse(input: string | string[]): object | object[] {
  // ONE implementation covering ALL signatures
  return [];
}

// THE SPECIAL RETURNS
let cb: () => void = () => 42;        // LEGAL: void = return ignored
function fail(msg: string): never {   // never = does NOT return
  throw new Error(msg);
}
// exhaustiveness — the pattern:
switch (shape.kind) {
  case 'circle': return areaCircle(shape);
  case 'square': return areaSquare(shape);
  default: {
    const _exhaustive: never = shape;  // NEW kind at compile = ERROR here
    return _exhaustive;
  }
}
```

The generic discipline (the part that proves understanding): a generic is a RELATIONSHIP, not a label — T must connect at least two positions (a param and the return, the container and its element) or it's decorative. When you need capabilities from T (access a property, call methods), the CONSTRAINT provides them: T extends { id: string } both narrows callers and lets the body read obj.id. keyof T + T[K] is the crown pattern — the function adapts to ANY object while returning EXACT types, and the call-site sees 'number' not a union.

Overload rules (when real): overloads shine when the SAME name maps different INPUTS to different OUTPUT types (parse(string) → object vs parse(string[]) → object[]). If a single union parameter describes it (function f(x: string | number): string), one signature wins — overloading for uniform behavior is ceremony. And the arrow-function trap: overloaded function TYPES compose with intersections; overloaded DECLARATIONS are statement-level only — pick per context.

Void vs undefined vs never (the trio interviewers probe): void on a CALLBACK means 'I ignore your return' — implementations MAY return values (the (e) => 42 as () => void case — by design, for operator callbacks like map/forEach); undefined means 'returns, possibly nothing'; never means the function does NOT return (throws/loops) — which is why never is the exhaustiveness sentinel: a default-branch value of never only type-checks when every case was handled.
